n, we pull it out of the lump and give it a label. In fact, the history of medicine is a long story of carving distinct diseases out of the blob of "fevers" or "catarrh." The blob that remains—the common cold—is the residue: the viruses that are so clinically mild and interchangeable that inventing 200 separate names would serve no purpose except to confuse everyone. So the lack of individual names for the 200 cold viruses isn't evidence that they don't exist. It's evidence that **the illness they share is so uniform, and so mild, that our medical and linguistic systems rightly treat them as a single entity for practical purposes.** The virus matters far less than what it does to you. And what it does to you, over and over, is exactly the same tedious symphony, no matter who's waving the baton.
